/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 65 0 R
/T (The Airline Reservation System demonstrates how a real-world airline application can be represented using a relational database. The system divides information into meaningful tables such as Passenger, Airport, Aircraft, Flight, Seat, Booking, Ticket, Payment, and Baggage. Primary keys uniquely identify records, while foreign keys establish relationships between different tables. The Booking table plays an important role in connecting passengers with flights and resolving the many-to-many relationship between them. The database design helps reduce data duplication, maintain consistency, and efficiently manage flight reservations, passenger information, seat selection, payments, tickets, and baggage.)
/E (The Airline Reservation System demonstrates how a real-world airline application can be represented using a relational database. The system divides information into meaningful tables such as Passenger, Airport, Aircraft, Flight, Seat, Booking, Ticket, Payment, and Baggage. Primary keys uniquely identify records, while foreign keys establish relationships between different tables. The Booking table plays an important role in connecting passengers with flights and resolving the many-to-many relationship between them. The database design helps reduce data duplication, maintain consistency, and efficiently manage flight reservations, passenger information, seat selection, payments, tickets, and baggage.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
